WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:40.370
[MUSIC]

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.551
Please be seated.

00:08:59.551 --> 00:09:04.179
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:08.553 --> 00:09:11.119
&gt;&gt; Graduates, honored guests,

00:09:11.119 --> 00:09:14.670
faculty staff, families, and friends,

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:20.401
welcome to the long awaited Class
of 2020 Graduation Ceremony.

00:09:20.401 --> 00:09:30.914
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:30.914 --> 00:09:37.021
&gt;&gt; At the Stanford University
Graduate School of Business.

00:09:37.021 --> 00:09:41.836
Two years ago, as you prepared for
graduation, we went online and

00:09:41.836 --> 00:09:44.423
cancelled all in-person events.

00:09:44.423 --> 00:09:47.804
Similar decisions were
made around the world,

00:09:47.804 --> 00:09:52.883
later around that time, Queen Elizabeth
spoke to the British people.

00:09:52.883 --> 00:09:59.462
Her theme as we confronted the growing
pandemic was perseverance.

00:09:59.462 --> 00:10:06.925
She said, Well, we have faced challenges
before, this one is different.

00:10:06.925 --> 00:10:11.441
We should take comfort that
while we may have more

00:10:11.441 --> 00:10:15.745
still to endure, better days we'll return.

00:10:15.745 --> 00:10:18.792
We will be with our friends again,

00:10:18.792 --> 00:10:23.743
we will be with our families again,
we will meet again.

00:10:23.743 --> 00:10:29.824
Today class of 2020 We meet again.

00:10:29.824 --> 00:10:38.876
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:10:38.876 --> 00:10:44.109
I am thrilled that nearly 400
of you are here to celebrate.

00:10:44.109 --> 00:10:49.802
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Let's hear it for

00:10:49.802 --> 00:10:52.733
your perseverance, GSB class of 2020.

00:10:52.733 --> 00:11:00.312
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:11:00.312 --> 00:11:02.983
&gt;&gt; I'd like to take a moment to recognize

00:11:02.983 --> 00:11:06.070
some of the people here
today GSB faculty and

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:11.091
staff played a pivotal role in
the experience of the class of 2020.

00:11:11.091 --> 00:11:17.282
Could I ask GSB faculty and
staff to stand for a moment of thanks.

00:11:17.282 --> 00:11:27.126
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:11:27.126 --> 00:11:29.163
&gt;&gt; We also are joined by families and

00:11:29.163 --> 00:11:33.249
friends who have traveled to
celebrate this important occasion.

00:11:33.249 --> 00:11:38.018
Let's turn around and take a moment to
recognize our families and friends.

00:11:38.018 --> 00:11:48.526
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:11:48.526 --> 00:11:51.085
&gt;&gt; A typical graduation is a moment of

00:11:51.085 --> 00:11:56.291
transition, we close the door on
the experience of being a student and

00:11:56.291 --> 00:12:00.018
open a new one as graduates
launch into the world.

00:12:00.018 --> 00:12:06.029
Precisely because graduates are so close
to being students, it is hard to fully

00:12:06.029 --> 00:12:11.251
appreciate what has been learned and
what the experience has meant.

00:12:11.251 --> 00:12:16.844
Today's ceremony is different,
rather than a transition,

00:12:16.844 --> 00:12:21.045
let's conceive it as
a moment of inflection.

00:12:21.045 --> 00:12:25.832
&gt;&gt; Have an opportunity to look
back on being a GSB student and

00:12:25.832 --> 00:12:30.124
how that experience has
shaped your trajectory and

00:12:30.124 --> 00:12:32.735
also to look out to the future.

00:12:32.735 --> 00:12:37.714
The Danish philosopher
Soren Kierkegaard famously wrote

00:12:37.714 --> 00:12:42.399
that life is understood backward and
lived forwards.

00:12:42.399 --> 00:12:47.290
Class of 2020,
you are in a unique position

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:51.460
to appreciate what Kierkegaard meant.

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:54.844
As you sit here at graduation,

00:12:54.844 --> 00:13:00.049
you have the benefit of
two years to look back and

00:13:00.049 --> 00:13:05.388
consider what part of being
a student opened your

00:13:05.388 --> 00:13:10.227
mind changed you influenced your choices.

00:13:10.227 --> 00:13:15.352
I expect that many specifics have faded,
a has settled

00:13:15.352 --> 00:13:20.479
on the details of accrual accounting,
simulation modeling,

00:13:20.479 --> 00:13:25.335
how much you paid for
the furniture in your past down house.

00:13:28.864 --> 00:13:34.682
Instead, moments of serendipity
take on larger meaning,

00:13:34.682 --> 00:13:40.614
an illuminating common in a class,
in a memorable speaker,

00:13:40.614 --> 00:13:44.937
a conversation that ignited a friendship.

00:13:44.937 --> 00:13:49.354
I also hope that as a result
of your GSB experience,

00:13:49.354 --> 00:13:55.641
you think more clearly about decision
making strategy organizations.

00:13:55.641 --> 00:14:00.366
I hope you've built on the foundation
of leadership skills you've developed.

00:14:00.366 --> 00:14:05.329
I hope the relationships you
formed have become stronger and

00:14:05.329 --> 00:14:11.473
I hope returning this weekend provides
an opportunity to reflect on this.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:18.917
When Kierkegaard wrote that life must
be lived forward, he meant that not

00:14:18.917 --> 00:14:23.694
everything could be anticipated or
planned or understood in advance.

00:14:23.694 --> 00:14:27.133
Sometimes you just have to make it up,

00:14:27.133 --> 00:14:32.082
you learn that lesson in your
final quarter at the GSB.

00:14:32.082 --> 00:14:35.623
You started nonprofits to
source medical supplies,

00:14:35.623 --> 00:14:40.511
you organized dozens of virtual events
to maintain a sense of community.

00:14:40.511 --> 00:14:45.698
You work to address racial inequality,
you're regenerative,

00:14:45.698 --> 00:14:51.354
in the face of bad news and uncertainty,
you figured out how to create,

00:14:51.354 --> 00:14:58.338
how to build, even had a contagiously
spread positivity We've watched

00:14:58.338 --> 00:15:03.697
you carry that into your careers, if
today's graduation is an inflection point,

00:15:03.697 --> 00:15:08.377
we should recognize that we are also
at an inflection point in the world.

00:15:08.377 --> 00:15:12.380
We are gradually emerging
from two years of pandemic,

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:16.305
the world has changed and
we face urgent challenges.

00:15:18.545 --> 00:15:25.098
My final message to you in June of 2020,
I quoted President Franklin Roosevelt,

00:15:25.098 --> 00:15:29.375
who in the Great Depression
told the graduating class.

00:15:29.375 --> 00:15:35.359
Yours is not the task of making
your way in the world but

00:15:35.359 --> 00:15:40.546
the task of remaking the world,
which you will

00:15:40.546 --> 00:15:46.014
find before you,
that still resonates with me.

00:15:46.014 --> 00:15:50.238
As you renew your Stanford ties today and
relaunch,

00:15:50.238 --> 00:15:55.710
remember that each of you has
the capacity not just to make your way.

00:15:55.710 --> 00:16:00.658
Rise high,
build in a way that raises others set

00:16:00.658 --> 00:16:06.620
yourselves the task of making
a better world around you.

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:13.954
Welcome back to Stanford and
congratulations.

00:16:13.954 --> 00:16:21.481
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:16:21.481 --> 00:16:24.188
&gt;&gt; Now it's my great pleasure to introduce

00:16:24.188 --> 00:16:26.262
our speaker, Orlando Bravo.

00:16:26.262 --> 00:16:31.912
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Orlando was born

00:16:31.912 --> 00:16:37.055
in Puerto Rico- As a teenager, he became
a nationally ranked junior tennis player,

00:16:37.055 --> 00:16:41.317
at one point even roomed with the future
world number one, Jim Courier.

00:16:41.317 --> 00:16:45.626
He went to Brown University, and
after a short stint on Wall Street,

00:16:45.626 --> 00:16:46.823
came to Stanford.

00:16:46.823 --> 00:16:52.174
He earned both an MBA and
a JD from the Stanford Law School.

00:16:52.174 --> 00:16:55.266
Orlando went on in finance and
private equity.

00:16:55.266 --> 00:17:01.528
And in 2008, he founded Thoma Bravo,
with fellow GSB alum, Carl Toma.

00:17:01.528 --> 00:17:03.101
Under Orlando's leadership,

00:17:03.101 --> 00:17:06.078
Thoma Bravo has become a leading
software investment firm.

00:17:06.078 --> 00:17:07.652
And a few years ago,

00:17:07.652 --> 00:17:12.759
Forbes magazine called
Orlando Wall Street's best deal maker.

00:17:12.759 --> 00:17:16.201
Orlando returned to Puerto Rico
after Hurricane Maria

00:17:16.201 --> 00:17:18.753
in 2017 to lead humanitarian work.

00:17:18.753 --> 00:17:23.303
Since then, the Bravo Family Foundation
has focused its efforts on creating

00:17:23.303 --> 00:17:26.174
opportunities for
young adults in Puerto Rico.

00:17:26.174 --> 00:17:31.638
Orlando was a trustee of Brown University
and a great supporter of the GSB,

00:17:31.638 --> 00:17:37.120
and as you're about to see, his
brilliance and his energy are legendary.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:39.932
Please join me in welcoming Orlando Bravo.

00:17:39.932 --> 00:17:45.315
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you.

00:17:45.315 --> 00:17:47.417
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:17:47.417 --> 00:17:53.628
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:17:53.628 --> 00:17:59.535
&gt;&gt; GSB class of 2020, let's go.

00:17:59.535 --> 00:18:01.221
Seriously, and by the way,

00:18:01.221 --> 00:18:04.532
that's something that I say
when I'm really fired up.

00:18:04.532 --> 00:18:08.909
So remember that, because I'm going to
ask you to say it at the end, okay?

00:18:08.909 --> 00:18:13.702
And I'll be brief, but I'm really so
excited for all of you.

00:18:13.702 --> 00:18:19.888
You're here together, you
are the absolute best of your generation.

00:18:19.888 --> 00:18:24.336
And you have come together
here to celebrate as

00:18:24.336 --> 00:18:29.366
fellow members of our very
close GSB alumni family.

00:18:29.366 --> 00:18:33.082
Now, I'm going to talk
to you from the heart,

00:18:33.082 --> 00:18:39.123
because I know no other way to really
address you and really talk to you.

00:18:39.123 --> 00:18:43.601
It is so meaningful for
me to be here with you today,

00:18:43.601 --> 00:18:48.825
because Stanford has had such
a profound impact on my life.

00:18:48.825 --> 00:18:55.436
The GSB is the place that gave me the
courage and the confidence to think big,

00:18:55.436 --> 00:19:00.614
while at the same time encouraging
me to celebrate my past.

00:19:00.614 --> 00:19:05.179
So here I am with an incredible
chance to speak with all of you, and

00:19:05.179 --> 00:19:10.330
I want to thank Dean Levin for giving
me this just incredible opportunity.

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:15.876
I'm going to tell you a brief
story of how I got to Stanford.

00:19:15.876 --> 00:19:21.320
And the purpose of my story is
that I think it best exemplifies

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:26.145
the culture here at the GSB
that all of you represent.

00:19:26.145 --> 00:19:33.673
Exactly 30 years ago,
I got into Stanford, yes, I did.

00:19:33.673 --> 00:19:38.372
And I also got into some, I'm just
going to say it, east coast school,

00:19:38.372 --> 00:19:40.410
all right, at the same time.

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:45.766
Now, late in my decision making process,
I was able to get this job.

00:19:45.766 --> 00:19:48.818
I didn't know I was going to get
this job offer, but I got it.

00:19:48.818 --> 00:19:50.700
And I really wanted to do that for

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:53.782
a couple of years before
coming to business school.

00:19:53.782 --> 00:19:58.704
So I first called the school on the east
coast to see how they would feel about me

00:19:58.704 --> 00:20:00.610
deferring a couple of years.

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:05.644
And their answer was really clear,
absolutely not.

00:20:05.644 --> 00:20:09.428
If you don't reply by this date,
you will lose your spot.

00:20:09.428 --> 00:20:14.321
And if you would like to come years later,
maybe you should apply again.

00:20:14.321 --> 00:20:18.183
With my hopes down,
I then called Stanford.

00:20:18.183 --> 00:20:21.690
And on that call, the head of admissions
just happened to be in her office.

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:27.107
She picked up the line, and
I scrambled to start making my case.

00:20:27.107 --> 00:20:31.579
And as I did, she stopped me,
she said, Orlando,

00:20:31.579 --> 00:20:35.436
you can come to Stanford
whenever you want.

00:20:35.436 --> 00:20:39.425
We will always have a place here for you.

00:20:39.425 --> 00:20:46.302
Just give us about six months advance
notice and let us know when you're ready.

00:20:46.302 --> 00:20:51.840
Wow, I will forever be grateful for
that call.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:57.620
Stanford welcomed me with open arms,
and even before I set foot on campus,

00:20:57.620 --> 00:21:01.840
Stanford was encouraging me
to do what I wanted to do and

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:04.342
it was opening big doors for me.

00:21:04.342 --> 00:21:08.762
Now, I share this story because
it's a good one for me.

00:21:08.762 --> 00:21:13.399
But the point of me sharing
that with you is on that call,

00:21:13.399 --> 00:21:18.037
the head of admissions at
the time exemplified three key

00:21:18.037 --> 00:21:22.688
traits from your culture,
our culture here at the GSB.

00:21:22.688 --> 00:21:25.997
First, she was a real person.

00:21:25.997 --> 00:21:27.595
She spoke to me like a real person.

00:21:27.595 --> 00:21:30.826
She was genuine, she was authentic.

00:21:30.826 --> 00:21:33.770
Two, she was relaxed.

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:35.805
She was casual.

00:21:35.805 --> 00:21:40.600
And third,
she was focused on me being ready.

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:45.935
My goal today is to inspire
all of you to carry with you

00:21:45.935 --> 00:21:52.984
throughout your journey these three
key cultural traits of the GSB.

00:21:52.984 --> 00:21:58.906
Number one, be real,
number two, stay relaxed,

00:21:58.906 --> 00:22:04.437
and number three,
be ready, be ready, okay?

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:11.591
Let's talk about being real for a second.

00:22:11.591 --> 00:22:17.368
When I first came to the GSB,
I knew I was coming to the place with

00:22:17.368 --> 00:22:23.363
the absolute best classmates and
the most impressive scholars,

00:22:23.363 --> 00:22:28.923
but little did I know how much
fun this place would be, okay?

00:22:28.923 --> 00:22:33.035
We found roommates and I lived with seven
of them and we became best friends.

00:22:33.035 --> 00:22:38.496
By the way, they're here in the crowd
in very special seats after many years.

00:22:38.496 --> 00:22:41.325
And we threw one party after the other.

00:22:41.325 --> 00:22:44.000
I am sorry, but we did, okay?

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:46.320
We would go to Cabo.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:50.862
And I believe it was my class
that started a tradition of

00:22:50.862 --> 00:22:55.622
trying to rally as many people
as possible to go to Vegas.

00:22:55.622 --> 00:23:00.925
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Now,

00:23:00.925 --> 00:23:04.957
I also understand that all of
you have upped your game, and

00:23:04.957 --> 00:23:09.730
now the entire class goes to Vegas,
which proves one of my theories,

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:14.037
that the younger generation is
always better than the prior.

00:23:14.037 --> 00:23:16.285
But anyway, within this,

00:23:16.285 --> 00:23:21.857
Stanford combines an environment in
the GSB of the best of the best,

00:23:21.857 --> 00:23:26.184
while at the same time,
a very fun and casual place.

00:23:26.184 --> 00:23:32.501
And that in itself is such an important
lesson in leadership and in life.

00:23:32.501 --> 00:23:39.441
Think about it, you can be the highest
performing individual while staying real,

00:23:39.441 --> 00:23:43.717
while staying authentic,
while being yourself.

00:23:43.717 --> 00:23:48.810
And when you do that,
your personal story will shine so

00:23:48.810 --> 00:23:56.130
much better, and it will for sure make
you a much, much, much better leader.

00:23:56.130 --> 00:24:02.156
Number two,
Sorry that I have to drink water,

00:24:02.156 --> 00:24:04.804
I'm getting fired up about this.

00:24:04.804 --> 00:24:09.710
Number two, I want you all to work
really hard in staying relaxed.

00:24:10.830 --> 00:24:14.570
There's so much pressure now,
in being calm.

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:16.465
You're the best.

00:24:16.465 --> 00:24:19.503
Why worry so much?

00:24:19.503 --> 00:24:22.255
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Really.

00:24:22.255 --> 00:24:30.573
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:24:30.573 --> 00:24:36.627
&gt;&gt; When I graduated, From the GSB in 1998.

00:24:36.627 --> 00:24:39.133
I got my dream job,
a dream job in private equity.

00:24:39.133 --> 00:24:42.797
I've been trying to get into
the industry for a long time.

00:24:42.797 --> 00:24:48.648
But then I proceeded to set
strict deadlines on my progress.

00:24:48.648 --> 00:24:52.993
I put just incredible pressure on myself.

00:24:52.993 --> 00:24:54.807
I worked so hard.

00:24:54.807 --> 00:24:59.221
And three years later,
I had absolutely nothing to show for it,

00:24:59.221 --> 00:25:02.756
except for
bad deals that we're all going to zero.

00:25:02.756 --> 00:25:07.162
They really weren't which was
a disaster and I was about to be fired.

00:25:07.162 --> 00:25:11.105
At the same time,
my first daughter was born.

00:25:11.105 --> 00:25:13.443
I remember this like yesterday.

00:25:13.443 --> 00:25:15.449
I'm there at the hospital.

00:25:15.449 --> 00:25:18.671
And I'm thinking I forgot to buy diapers.

00:25:18.671 --> 00:25:21.663
So I rushed over to the Marina Safeway.

00:25:21.663 --> 00:25:24.055
And by the way for
all of you that live in San Francisco,

00:25:24.055 --> 00:25:26.454
please don't go to
the Marina Safeway to buy diapers.

00:25:26.454 --> 00:25:27.910
You go there to meet people.

00:25:27.910 --> 00:25:32.169
It's an excellent environment for
that, but don't go there for that.

00:25:32.169 --> 00:25:39.132
So I find myself in this, I guess,
diaper aisle, thinking, what did I do?

00:25:39.132 --> 00:25:41.330
What just happened to me?

00:25:41.330 --> 00:25:46.744
From the days of the GSB of thinking big,
of going to Vegas, with my

00:25:46.744 --> 00:25:53.418
classmates to now about to get fired I was
completely unprepared to have a child and

00:25:53.418 --> 00:25:58.376
fumbling through a diaper aisle
here at the Marina Safeway.

00:25:58.376 --> 00:26:03.444
And then I promise you
something switched for me.

00:26:03.444 --> 00:26:05.959
I started laughing.

00:26:05.959 --> 00:26:08.312
I accepted the moment.

00:26:08.312 --> 00:26:10.494
I let it go.

00:26:10.494 --> 00:26:12.620
It all changed.

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:15.582
I relaxed, I calm down.

00:26:15.582 --> 00:26:18.835
Two weeks later, I go back to work.

00:26:18.835 --> 00:26:24.081
And my mentor, Carl Toma, also GSB alumni,
one of the greatest of all time,

00:26:24.081 --> 00:26:28.306
he sits me down and said,
I'm going to give you a second chance.

00:26:28.306 --> 00:26:33.930
I was calm I was relaxed,
I was at peace and that gave me clarity,

00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:38.616
it gave me purpose and
allowed me to do my job with my own

00:26:38.616 --> 00:26:43.513
personal style which resulted
in fewer bad decisions,

00:26:43.513 --> 00:26:47.809
and better outcomes and
it was a better journey.

00:26:47.809 --> 00:26:54.440
To do something really important and
to do something that is very personal so

00:26:54.440 --> 00:27:00.448
it is for you to do you don't have to
go looking around for this event or

00:27:00.448 --> 00:27:06.995
what this moment is it will come to you
for some of you that are already here.

00:27:06.995 --> 00:27:14.279
Okay, the world will call upon you
to do something very important.

00:27:14.279 --> 00:27:15.262
You're the best.

00:27:15.262 --> 00:27:19.078
That means you will be
afforded a good journey, but

00:27:19.078 --> 00:27:23.961
because you're the best,
you'll have a big responsibility and

00:27:23.961 --> 00:27:27.887
you cannot opt-out,
out of that responsibility.

00:27:27.887 --> 00:27:31.834
For me, an important moment came in 2017.

00:27:31.834 --> 00:27:35.862
I was coming back from
a fundraising trip to Asia.

00:27:35.862 --> 00:27:40.511
And as the flight was going,
Hurricane Maria hit Puerto Rico.

00:27:40.511 --> 00:27:44.136
When I landed,
I knew things were really bad.

00:27:44.136 --> 00:27:46.434
I cannot get in touch with family,

00:27:46.434 --> 00:27:51.346
I cannot get in touch with all my friends
I get home and I'm glued to the TV,

00:27:51.346 --> 00:27:54.992
listening to a lot of reports
on how aid was not coming to

00:27:54.992 --> 00:27:59.695
the small towns right where I grew
up in because of logistical issues.

00:27:59.695 --> 00:28:04.562
The only communication that I got
during that time was when my reporter

00:28:04.562 --> 00:28:07.709
said there was a shelter
near where I grew up.

00:28:07.709 --> 00:28:11.755
That had just 24 hours of food and
water left.

00:28:11.755 --> 00:28:14.323
And that's when I knew I had my call.

00:28:14.323 --> 00:28:19.552
At that moment that was a call for me
because if I didn't do anything about it,

00:28:19.552 --> 00:28:21.891
no one would do anything about it.

00:28:21.891 --> 00:28:24.578
Now, I didn't know anything,

00:28:24.578 --> 00:28:29.762
about hurricane relief about
supply chains, I do software.

00:28:31.497 --> 00:28:35.542
About logistics, I didn't have any
contacts in the government of Puerto Rico,

00:28:35.542 --> 00:28:39.291
I didn't have any contacts with
community organizations in Puerto Rico,

00:28:39.291 --> 00:28:42.224
I lived in San Francisco,
I had friends and family there.

00:28:42.224 --> 00:28:44.373
But the GSB taught me to take a risk.

00:28:44.373 --> 00:28:47.056
And to not be afraid of failure.

00:28:47.056 --> 00:28:51.476
So we started a relief
operation GSB style.

00:28:51.476 --> 00:28:54.064
It was entrepreneurial.

00:28:54.064 --> 00:28:56.064
It was practical.

00:28:56.064 --> 00:28:57.715
It was immediate.

00:28:57.715 --> 00:29:00.344
It was results oriented.

00:29:00.344 --> 00:29:02.743
And within roughly 10 hours of that,

00:29:02.743 --> 00:29:07.260
we had communicated back to the reporter
to say we'll be there tomorrow.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:12.088
We had filled up this plane with as
much food and water as we could get.

00:29:12.088 --> 00:29:14.783
And we head off to Puerto Rico.

00:29:14.783 --> 00:29:21.190
And when we landed, this man opened the
doors of the plane with fear in his eyes.

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:24.771
As if to say I can't
believe somebody's here.

00:29:24.771 --> 00:29:27.503
The place was completely deserted.

00:29:27.503 --> 00:29:31.576
And then I looked to my left and
on the road, there were three trucks.

00:29:31.576 --> 00:29:32.840
They had made it.

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:34.220
We had made it.

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:37.630
And that's when I knew
I made the right call.

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:41.909
Now, that was my call,
you will get your own.

00:29:41.909 --> 00:29:46.806
And I'm really grateful for the GSB
because it led me to think that this

00:29:46.806 --> 00:29:52.118
incredible place didn't just train me
to be private equity professional.

00:29:52.118 --> 00:29:54.702
It trained me for
bigger moments like this one.

00:29:56.525 --> 00:30:00.694
I want to end by saying
that the GSB has given you

00:30:00.694 --> 00:30:05.209
a foundation to solve
some really big problems.

00:30:05.209 --> 00:30:10.742
Once again, you will be called upon
to do something really important and

00:30:10.742 --> 00:30:14.369
to do something that is very personal so
it is for

00:30:14.369 --> 00:30:18.993
you to do you don't have to go
looking around for this event or

00:30:18.993 --> 00:30:24.797
what this moment is it will come to you
for some of you that are already here for

00:30:24.797 --> 00:30:28.786
some others,
the moment will come a little sooner,

00:30:28.786 --> 00:30:32.089
for some other the moment will come later.

00:30:32.089 --> 00:30:36.990
But I promise you,
you will know exactly what that moment is,

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:39.922
and you will know exactly what to do.

00:30:39.922 --> 00:30:44.185
And when that happens, you have to go all
in, and you have to give it all you have.

00:30:47.234 --> 00:30:53.032
So, GSB class of 2020 I am
extremely proud of all of you.

00:30:53.032 --> 00:30:58.701
You will accomplish incredible things,
much bigger than those of your mentors.

00:30:58.701 --> 00:31:03.934
For now, remember and really remember,

00:31:03.934 --> 00:31:08.278
be real, be relaxed and be ready.

00:31:08.278 --> 00:31:13.530
And with that, I want you to repeat
it with me GSB class of 2020,

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:16.952
where we say, let's go one, two, three.

00:31:16.952 --> 00:31:18.122
Let's go.

00:31:18.122 --> 00:31:20.329
[CROSSTALK] Hey, there we go.

00:31:20.329 --> 00:31:30.329
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:34.737 --> 00:31:39.347
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, Orlando that
is a very tough act to follow.

00:31:39.347 --> 00:31:43.110
Good afternoon, I am so
delighted that we have set this up so

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:48.006
that the friends and family can get the
full experience of the GSB by sitting in

00:31:48.006 --> 00:31:50.217
the back with several empty rows.

00:31:50.217 --> 00:31:52.636
Between them and the speaker.

00:31:52.636 --> 00:31:56.291
That was a stroke of genius.

00:31:56.291 --> 00:32:01.908
It is my pleasure as director of
the Stanford MSX program to present

00:32:01.908 --> 00:32:08.230
the 2020 graduates who will receive
the degree of Master of Science and

00:32:08.230 --> 00:32:13.574
Management It is so great to see
you all back here and thriving.

00:32:13.574 --> 00:32:17.178
When we last saw you the pandemic
was just getting going.

00:32:17.178 --> 00:32:20.666
And many of you faced incredible
challenges here at Stanford and

00:32:20.666 --> 00:32:22.223
with your families at home.

00:32:22.223 --> 00:32:25.900
You finish the program under
a cloud of uncertainty and we're so

00:32:25.900 --> 00:32:29.655
proud of what you've all accomplished
in the intervening time.

00:32:29.655 --> 00:32:34.825
The mission of the MSX program is to is
to provide experienced leaders Men and

00:32:34.825 --> 00:32:39.024
women, who have spent at least
eight years in professional and

00:32:39.024 --> 00:32:43.384
management roles,
the opportunity to prepare themselves, for

00:32:43.384 --> 00:32:47.845
increasingly senior positions
in organizations they will lead.

00:32:47.845 --> 00:32:53.257
MSx students participate in a full time,
year long course of academic study,

00:32:53.257 --> 00:32:56.537
giving these students
an all too rare chance for

00:32:56.537 --> 00:33:01.469
reflection and growth in the midst
of an already accomplished career.

00:33:01.469 --> 00:33:05.086
On behalf of the administration
of the program and

00:33:05.086 --> 00:33:09.733
the faculty who have taught them,
join me in wishing each of them

00:33:09.733 --> 00:33:14.576
great fulfillment and success in
the next phase of their careers.

00:33:14.576 --> 00:33:20.289
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; In

00:33:20.289 --> 00:33:24.417
honor of the service of former GSB Dean,
Robert Joss,

00:33:24.417 --> 00:33:30.070
who was himself a student in this program,
the top 10% of the MSx class,

00:33:30.070 --> 00:33:35.668
ranked by academic performance
are designated as Robert Joss scholars.

00:33:35.668 --> 00:33:40.118
As we read the names of the graduates,
we will also announce if they have

00:33:40.118 --> 00:33:43.324
achieved the distinction
of Robert Joss Scholar.

00:33:43.324 --> 00:33:47.960
Margaret Hayes, Associate Dean of the MBA
and MSx Programs will now read our

00:33:47.960 --> 00:33:51.667
graduates names,
while Dean Levin presents the diplomas.

